.gpt-6-astra.stdout.log\\\\n?? collab/yang-clojure/1794163259000-architect-cc1-recheck-signed-ruling.prompt.md\\\\n?? collab/yang-clojure/1794170000000-architect-cc1-recheck-signed-ruling.findings.md\\\\n?? collab/yang-clojure/1794207465000-architect-cc1-recheck-r2.gpt-6-astra.stdout.log\\\\n?? collab/yang-clojure/1794207465000-architect-cc1-recheck-r2.prompt.md\\\\n?? collab/yang-clojure/1795060000000-architect-p1-amendments.diff\\\\n?? collab/yang-clojure/1795060000000-architect-p1-recheck.gpt-6-astra.stdout.log\\\\n?? collab/yang-clojure/1795220000000-reviewer-cc1-fix4.codex.stdout.log\\\\n?? collab/yang-clojure/1795310000000-owner-ruling-occurrence-identity-unitarity.md\\\\n?? collab/yang-clojure/1795315000000-architect-unitarity-ruling-recheck.gpt-6-astra.stdout.log\\\\n?? collab/yang-clojure/1795400000000-yangclojure-cc1-findings-and-lane-state-for-reassignment.md\\\\n?? collab/yang-clojure/1795450000000-architect-round5-u1-recheck.gpt-6-astra.stdout.log\\\\n?? collab/yang-clojure/1795455000000-reviewer-cc1-fix5.codex.stdout.log\\\\n?? collab/yang-php/1794101833000-engineer-php-e2e-diagnosis-r2.muse-wonder-1.0-r2.stdout.log\\\\n?? collab/yang-php/1794101833000-engineer-php-e2e-diagnosis-r2.muse-wonder-1.0.stdout.log\\\\n?? collab/yang-php/1794101833000-engineer-php-e2e-diagnosis-r2.prompt.md\\\\n?? collab/yang-php/1794166999000-engineer-php-echo-engine-regression.muse-wonder-1.0.stdout.log\\\\n?? collab/yang-php/1794166999000-engineer-php-echo-engine-regression.prompt.md\\\\n?? orchestrator-logs/1793952675-agy-gemini-3.8-flash-jolt-seat-takeover.md\\\\n?? orchestrator-logs/1794037099-zcode-glm-5.3-flash-exception-track-orchestrator-takeover.md\\\\n?? orchestrator-logs/1794147118-agy-gemini-3.8-flash-yin-repl-dht-refactor.md\\\\ngit: warning: confstr() failed with code 5: couldn't get path of DARWIN_USER_TEMP_DIR; using /tmp instead\\\\ngit: error: couldn't create cache file '/tmp/xcrun_db-Mqu5p2V7' (errno=Operation not permitted)\\\\n2026-11-09 13:58:17.068 xcodebuild[2953:118648183]  DVTFilePathFSEvents: Failed to start fs event stream.\\\\n2026-11-09 13:58:17.313 xcodebuild[2953:118648179] [MT] DVTDeveloperPaths: Failed to get length of DARWIN_USER_CACHE_DIR from confstr(3), error = Error Domain=NSPOSIXErrorDomain Code=5 \\\\\\\"Input/output error\\\\\\\". Using NSCachesDirectory instead.\\\\ngit: warning: confstr() failed with code 5: couldn't get path of DARWIN_USER_TEMP_DIR; using /tmp instead\\\\ngit: error: couldn't create cache file '/tmp/xcrun_db-hsdEzey6' (errno=Operation not permitted)\\\\n2026-11-09 13:58:18.185 xcodebuild[2959:118648232]  DVTFilePathFSEvents: Failed to start fs event stream.\\\\n2026-11-09 13:58:18.388 xcodebuild[2959:118648227] [MT] DVTDeveloperPaths: Failed to get length of DARWIN_USER_CACHE_DIR from confstr(3), error = Error Domain=NSPOSIXErrorDomain Code=5 \\\\\\\"Input/output error\\\\\\\". Using NSCachesDirectory instead.\\\\nb7dc23c494ca961a5a01cde31c2064a90e5a8534\\\\n---\\\\ndescription: Core philosophy, foundational axioms, invariants, primitives (tuples, streams, host boundaries), architectural commitments, design principles, code style, and development workflow for datom.world\\\\n---\\\\n\\\\n# DATOM.WORLD ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS & DEVELOPMENT RULES\\\\n\\\\n## Core Philosophy\\\\n\\\\nEverything is data.\\\\nInterpretation > abstraction.\\\\nExplicit causality > implicit assumptions.\\\\nRestrictions are a feature.\\\\n\\\\n### Foundational Axioms\\\\n\\\\n1. **Everything is a Stream**: Portable I/O and system data flow through append-only streams; hosts may also install explicit local functions that perform direct I/O.\\\\n2. **Interpretation Creates Semantics**: Data is syntax; semantics only emerge through interpretation (one truth, many perspectives).\\\\n   At system scale, a \\\\\\\"system\\\\\\\" is an emergent property of interpreters of\\\\n   pure data, not an object, service, or stored artifact. Find the\\\\n   interpreters and the data they share to find the system.\\\\n3. **Code and State are Datoms**: Code (Universal AST) and runtime execution state are structured datoms, enabling syntax independence and structural reflection.\\\\n4. **Everything is a Continuation**: Computation is modeled as serializable, portable continuations that can pause, travel across streams, and resume anywhere.\\\\n\\\\n## Non-Negotiable Invariants\\\\n\\\\n- Do not introduce hidden global state.\\\\n- Do not introduce implicit control flow.\\\\n- Do not introduce callbacks; every callback is an event on a stream.\\\\n- Do not introduce shared mutable state.\\\\n- Do not collapse interpretation and execution into the same layer.\\\\n- Do not assume graphs: graphs must be constructed explicitly from tuples.\\\\n- Do not lose information (unitarity): streams are append-only and no datom is destroyed, only reinterpreted. The Universal AST is never lost: it is always retrievable wherever code is lowered, by address or by a query over its datoms. Every derived form (semantic vector, native images, indexes) is an interpretation of it and may drop facts only because the AST remains.\\\\n\\\\n## Tuples and Datoms\\\\n\\\\nTuples are elements in an open moduli space, graded by dimension $n$. A tuple can be any dimension/size.\\\\nA datom is the canonical persistent tuple `[e a v t m]` (entity, attribute, value, transaction, metadata). Persistent facts are immutable datoms.\\\\nSource scope belongs to the interpreter, never to an appended tuple slot. Queries keep physical sources as separate db-values and express union or joins explicitly.\\\\n\\\\nSee [`docs/design/datom.md`](./datom.md) for full specification.\\\\n\\\\n## Streams\\\\n\\\\n`dao.stream` is an I/O monad and the system's portable I/O ABI. Functions consume streams and produce streams.\\\\nHosts may install arbitrary local functions, including direct I/O; their availability and migration semantics must be explicit.\\\\nPortable behavior cannot assume them; portable side effects appear as stream emissions. Direct-I/O host modules are explicitly local and non-migratable (owner decision, 2026-10-23).\\\\nStreams are values that can be sent through streams.\\\\nStreams carry whatever values the consumer needs: no datom requirement, no privileged payload shape.\\\\n\\\\nA stream has no privileged reader, and its meaning is not in it. Any number\\\\nof interpreters may observe the same medium, and each constructs its own\\\\nsemantics from the same batches: a `yin.vm` evaluator observing a program\\\\nstream constructs CESK state and executes it; `dao.space.index` observing\\\\nthe very same stream materializes covered indexes, so `dao.space.query/q`\\\\nanswers Datalog over the program as it grows. Neither knows the other\\\\nexists. The medium is their only coupling, and each observer's ignorance of\\\\nthe other is load-bearing: the evaluator never consults an index to run,\\\\nand the index never evaluates a form to index it. This is how axiom 2\\\\nbecomes architecture: one stream, many interpretations, none of them\\\\nthe stream's own.\\\\n\\\\nFunctions can take anything and return anything.\\\\nAgents are functions, closures, or continuations in `yin.vm` that communicate portably with the outside world through `dao.stream`. Portable agent behavior is specified through stream effects; host-local functions may also perform direct I/O.\\\\n\\\\nSee [`docs/design/dao.stream.md`](./dao.stream.md) for full stream specification.\\\\nSee [`docs/design/yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md`](./yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md) for VM architecture.\\\\n\\\\n### Host Boundaries\\\\n\\\\nA host boundary is the dao.stream abstraction boundary; implementations do not add contracts.\\\\n[datom.world.abi.md](./datom.world.abi.md) owns the doctrine, owner rulings, taxonomy, floor and custody rules.\\\\n\\\\n## Architectural Commitments\\\\n\\\\n- **`dao.jing` is syntax, agents are semantics**: the content-addressed encoding stays passive and payload-agnostic.\\\\n- **Macros are stream topology**: an evaluator knows nothing about macro expansion; expansion happens on the syntax side of a medium boundary, upstream of any evaluator.\\\\n- **Peer observers, one stream**: a generic evaluator and `dao.space` independently observe the same stream topic, as described under Streams. This is not a claim about any two consumers regardless of what they consume.\\\\n- **Observers are independent and optional**: nothing requires a medium to have any particular set of consumers. Which observers exist for a stream is a per-composition choice, not a design-time commitment.\\\\n\\\\n## Design Principles\\\\n\\\\nCode quality is measured by malleability: how cheaply can changes adapt without breaking promises?\\\\nStigmergic coordination emerges from sharing data across streams.\\\\n\\\\n**Systems emerge from interpreters of pure data.** Streams are the\\\\ncomposition medium; each interpreter gives the same data a perspective\\\\nwithout collapsing interpretation into execution or owning the truth.\\\\n\\\\n- `dao.space` emerges from `dao.space.index` and `dao.space.query` over\\\\n  ordinary streams.\\\\n- ShiBi capability, metering, and budget emerge from different\\\\n  interpreters over the same tuples; no token object is stored.\\\\n- The four VMs interpret one Universal AST; many syntaxes feed that AST.\\\\n- The semantic AST in `dao.space` is queryable facts projected by the\\\\n  auto-index observer.\\\\n- Client and server are roles in one stigmergic behavior implemented by\\\\n  interpreters, not distinct kinds of nodes.\\\\n\\\\n**Derive, don't persist.** Before adding a tag, slot, or field to any\\\\ncanonical content-hashed structure (an AST grammar, a datom schema, a row\\\\nshape), check whether a correctly scoped Datalog query over\\\\n`dao.space.query/q` can already compute the fact. If it can, it belongs as a\\\\nquery or projection: new persisted structure migrates every downstream\\\\nconsumer and duplicates authority over a fact a query derives fresh. The\\\\nretired `:global` tag in\\\\n[`docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md`](./yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md) is the\\\\ncase study, including the scoping bug that a naive query introduced.\\\\n\\\\nSee [`docs/agents/malleability.md`](../agents/malleability.md) for complete design guidance (cohesion, coupling, bounded complexity, evolution, testing strategy).\\\\nSee [`docs/agents/vocabulary.md`](../agents/vocabulary.md) for domain vocabulary reference (biology, economics, physics, philosophy).\\\\n\\\\n## Code Style\\\\n\\\\n- Prefer simple data over rich types.\\\\n- Prefer total functions over partial ones.\\\\n- Prefer declarative pipelines over imperative logic.\\\\n- Avoid cleverness.\\\\n- Name things after what they do to streams.\\\\n\\\\n## Development Workflow\\\\n\\\\n- Do not invent abstractions that hide streams.\\\\n- Do not suggest mainstream frameworks unless explicitly asked.\\\\n- Do not optimize prematurely.\\\\n- When uncertain, ask at the architectural level, not the implementation level.\\\\n- Explanations should align with Plan 9 / Datomic / Lisp lineage.\\\\n- Program store writes go through `engine/store-put`; any other store write\\\\n  the store-write audit (`test/yin/vm/store_write_audit_test.clj`)\\\\n  detects must be on its allowlist; its docstring states the residual it\\\\n  leaves to review.\\\\n\\\\n### Problem Decomposition\\\\n\\\\nIf a problem appears complex:\\\\n1. Decompose it into streams.\\\\n2. Identify invariants.\\\\n3. Restrict degrees of freedom.\\\\n4. Make causality explicit.\\\\n5. Let structure emerge from constraints.\\\\n\\\\n## Operational & Specialized Guides\\\\n\\\\n- **Advanced Concepts**: See [`docs/agents/advanced-concepts.md`](../agents/advanced-concepts.md) for parallel transport, capability tokens, and entanglement.\\\\n- **Build & Test**: See [`docs/agents/build-n-test.md`](../agents/build-n-test.md) for build, lint, and test commands and TDD guidelines.\\\\n- **Engineering Team Roster**: See [`docs/agents/team.md`](../agents/team.md) for engineering team roles and LLM model assignments.\\\\n- **Agent Delegation**: See [`docs/agents/roles/orchestrator.md`](../agents/roles/orchestrator.md) for collaboration protocols and delegating tasks or reviews through `agy`, `cmd`, `glm`, `deepseek`, and `codex`; each orchestrator session has its own append-only log at `orchestrator-logs/<unix-time-stamp>-<brief-orchestrator-descriptive-name>.md` under the repository root.\\\\n- **Website & Content**: See [`docs/agents/format.md`](../agents/format.md) for `.chp` and `.blog` EDN/Hiccup file specifications.\\\\n\\\",\\\"exit_code\\\":0,\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}}\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1794207465000-architect-cc1-recheck-r2.gpt-6-astra.stdout.log:6:{\\\"type\\\":\\\"item.started\\\",\\\"item\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"item_2\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"command_execution\\\",\\\"command\\\":\\\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\\\\\"cat .agents/skills/code-review-pr/SKILL.md; cat collab/yang-clojure/1794163259000-architect-cc1-recheck-signed-ruling.findings.md; cat collab/yang-clojure/1794170000000-architect-cc1-recheck-signed-ruling.findings.md; rg -n 'metadata|new defect|quota|Completed|origin|with-meta' collab/yang-clojure/1794163259000-architect-cc1-recheck-signed-ruling.gpt-6-astra.stdout.log\\\\\\\"\\\",\\\"aggregated_output\\\":\\\"\\\",\\\"exit_code\\\":null,\\\"status\\\":\\\"in_progress\\\"}}\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1794207465000-architect-cc1-recheck-r2.gpt-6-astra.stdout.log:7:{\\\"type\\\":\\\"item.completed\\\",\\\"item\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"item_2\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"command_execution\\\",\\\"command\\\":\\\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\\\\\"cat .agents/skills/code-review-pr/SKILL.md; cat collab/yang-clojure/1794163259000-architect-cc1-recheck-signed-ruling.findings.md; cat collab/yang-clojure/1794170000000-architect-cc1-recheck-signed-ruling.findings.md; rg -n 'metadata|new defect|quota|Completed|origin|with-meta' collab/yang-clojure/1794163259000-architect-cc1-recheck-signed-ruling.gpt-6-astra.stdout.log\\\\\\\"\\\",\\\"aggregated_output\\\":\\\"---\\\\nname: code-review-pr\\\\ndescription: >-\\\\n  Performs comprehensive, high-quality code reviews on Git pull requests, branches, commit ranges, or local working tree diffs. Analyzes code correctness, security, architecture, test coverage, and performance using automated diff analysis and structured review checklists.\\\\n---\\\\n\\\\n# Code Review PR Skill\\\\n\\\\n## Overview\\\\n\\\\nThe `code-review-pr` skill provides an end-to-end framework for conducting rigorous, actionable code reviews on Git pull requests, feature branches, commit ranges, or working tree diffs. It combines automated diff scanning via `scripts/pr_reviewer.py` with structured review checklists across security, correctness, architecture, performance, and testing.\\\\n\\\\nUse this skill whenever asked to:\\\\n- Review a pull request or feature branch (`git diff main...HEAD`).\\\\n- Review recent commits (`git diff HEAD~1` or commit range).\\\\n- Review uncommitted local changes (`git diff` or staged changes).\\\\n- Perform a security, quality, or architecture audit of proposed code changes.\\\\n\\\\n---\\\\n\\\\n## Quick Start\\\\n\\\\nRun the automated PR reviewer script to generate an initial risk assessment, churn metrics, and automated smell scan:\\\\n\\\\n```bash\\\\n# 1. Review branch changes against main (Default PR review)\\\\npython3 .agents/skills/code-review-pr/scripts/pr_reviewer.py analyze --target main...HEAD\\\\n\\\\n# 2. Review last commit\\\\npython3 .agents/skills/code-review-pr/scripts/pr_reviewer.py analyze --target HEAD~1\\\\n\\\\n# 3. Review uncommitted working tree changes\\\\npython3 .agents/skills/code-review-pr/scripts/pr_reviewer.py analyze --target working\\\\n\\\\n# 4. Save review report to file\\\\npython3 .agents/skills/code-review-pr/scripts/pr_reviewer.py analyze --target main...HEAD --output review_report.md\\\\n```\\\\n\\\\n---\\\\n\\\\n## Code Review Workflow\\\\n\\\\nFollow this step-by-step process when executing a code review:\\\\n\\\\n### Step 1: Run Automated Diff Analysis\\\\nExecute `scripts/pr_reviewer.py analyze` for the target Git ref/range. Review the output to determine:\\\\n- Total lines added/deleted and file churn distribution.\\\\n- High-risk modified files (e.g., Security/Auth, Database/Schema, Infrastructure, API Contracts).\\\\n- Test-to-source additions ratio.\\\\n- Automated static findings (hardcoded secrets, debug leftovers, swallowed exceptions, SQL injection risks).\\\\n\\\\n### Step 2: Deep-Dive Code Inspection\\\\nInspect the diffs for high-risk files using `git diff <target> -- <path>` or `view_file`. Focus on:\\\\n1. **Logic & Edge Cases**:\\\\n   - Boundary checks, null/nil handling, empty collections, zero division.\\\\n   - Resource leaks (unclosed streams, file handles, DB connections).\\\\n   - Race conditions, shared state mutation, concurrency locks.\\\\n2. **Security & Credentials**:\\\\n   - No hardcoded API keys, passwords, or tokens.\\\\n   - Input validation before execution or query interpolation.\\\\n   - Proper authorization checks on new or updated endpoints.\\\\n3. **Architecture & Contracts**:\\\\n   - Preserving backward compatibility on existing API functions/endpoints.\\\\n   - Proper module separation and clean interface boundaries.\\\\n4. **Test Coverage & Quality**:\\\\n   - Ensuring new source logic is accompanied by unit/integration tests.\\\\n   - Verifying tests validate actual assertions rather than just non-crashing execution.\\\\n\\\\n### Step 3: Reference Checklist & Severity Taxonomy\\\\nConsult [references/review_checklist.md](references/review_checklist.md) to classify findings:\\\\n- **P0 (Critical / Blocker)**: Security breaches, data loss risks, severe crashes, hardcoded credentials.\\\\n- **P1 (High / Must Address)**: Logic bugs, broken API contracts, swallowed exceptions, missing critical tests.\\\\n- **P2 (Medium / Recommended)**: Performance bottlenecks, code duplication, missing parameter validation.\\\\n- **P3 (Low / Suggestion)**: Minor formatting, naming improvements, leftover comments/TODOs.\\\\n\\\\n### Step 4: Formulate Structured Review Report\\\\nPresent findings to the user using the following template:\\\\n\\\\n```markdown\\\\n# 🔍 Code Review Report\\\\n\\\\n## Executive Summary\\\\n- **Target:** `<git-ref>`\\\\n- **Verdict:** `[ APPROVED | REQUEST CHANGES | COMMENT ]`\\\\n- **Overall Risk:** `[ LOW | MEDIUM | HIGH ]`\\\\n- **Metrics:** `X files changed, +Y / -Z lines`\\\\n\\\\n## 🚨 Critical Findings (P0 / P1)\\\\n- **[Severity]** `path/to/file.ext:L123` - Short issue description.\\\\n  ```diff\\\\n  - problematic code\\\\n  + suggested fix\\\\n  ```\\\\n\\\\n## 💡 Improvements & Suggestions (P2 / P3)\\\\n- **[Severity]** `path/to/file.ext` - Recommendation details.\\\\n\\\\n## 🧪 Test Coverage & Quality Assessment\\\\n- Evaluation of test ratio and edge case coverage.\\\\n\\\\n## 🌟 Positive Highlights\\\\n- Clean implementations, thorough documentation, or well-structured refactoring.\\\\n```\\\\n\\\\n---\\\\n\\\\n## Utility Script Reference\\\\n\\\\nThe bundled helper script `scripts/pr_reviewer.py` supports the following options:\\\\n\\\\n| Parameter | Type | Default | Description |\\\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\\\n| `--target` | String | `main...HEAD` | Git range/ref (`main...HEAD`, `HEAD~1`, `staged`, `working`) |\\\\n| `--output` | String | None | File path to write report output |\\\\n| `--format` | String | `markdown` | Output format (`markdown` or `json`) |\\\\n| `--repo-dir` | String | Current Dir | Path to the target Git repository |\\\\n\\\\n---\\\\n\\\\n## Common Review Pitfalls\\\\n\\\\n1. **Nit Tunnel Vision**: Focusing heavily on formatting or naming while missing underlying logic bugs or edge-case crashes.\\\\n2. **Ignoring Test Ratio**: Approving large source code changes without verifying corresponding test coverage additions.\\\\n3. **Swallowing Symptoms**: Suggesting `try/except` wrappers around failing code rather than addressing root cause invalid inputs or broken state.\\\\nThe signed ruling is present at `368297c3`, and the diff to `fa70516f` matches the stated 14 files and +2158/−116 lines. I’m tracing round 5’s construction provenance and publication custody against U1–U3.The signed ruling is present at `368297c3`, and the diff to `fa70516f` matches the stated 14 files and +2158/−116 lines. I’m tracing round 5’s construction provenance and publication custody against U1–U3.5:{\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"item.completed\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"item\\\\\\\":{\\\\\\\"id\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"item_1\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"command_execution\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"command\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/datom.world.md'\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"aggregated_output\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"---\\\\\\\\ndescription: Core philosophy, foundational axioms, invariants, primitives (tuples, streams, host boundaries), architectural commitments, design principles, code style, and development workflow for datom.world\\\\\\\\n---\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n# DATOM.WORLD ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS & DEVELOPMENT RULES\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n## Core Philosophy\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nEverything is data.\\\\\\\\nInterpretation > abstraction.\\\\\\\\nExplicit causality > implicit assumptions.\\\\\\\\nRestrictions are a feature.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n### Foundational Axioms\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n1. **Everything is a Stream**: Portable I/O and system data flow through append-only streams; hosts may also install explicit local functions that perform direct I/O.\\\\\\\\n2. **Interpretation Creates Semantics**: Data is syntax; semantics only emerge through interpretation (one truth, many perspectives).\\\\\\\\n   At system scale, a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"system\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" is an emergent property of interpreters of\\\\\\\\n   pure data, not an object, service, or stored artifact. Find the\\\\\\\\n   interpreters and the data they share to find the system.\\\\\\\\n3. **Code and State are Datoms**: Code (Universal AST) and runtime execution state are structured datoms, enabling syntax independence and structural reflection.\\\\\\\\n4. **Everything is a Continuation**: Computation is modeled as serializable, portable continuations that can pause, travel across streams, and resume anywhere.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n## Non-Negotiable Invariants\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n- Do not introduce hidden global state.\\\\\\\\n- Do not introduce implicit control flow.\\\\\\\\n- Do not introduce callbacks; every callback is an event on a stream.\\\\\\\\n- Do not introduce shared mutable state.\\\\\\\\n- Do not collapse interpretation and execution into the same layer.\\\\\\\\n- Do not assume graphs: graphs must be constructed explicitly from tuples.\\\\\\\\n- Do not lose information (unitarity): streams are append-only and no datom is destroyed, only reinterpreted. The Universal AST is never lost: it is always retrievable wherever code is lowered, by address or by a query over its datoms. Every derived form (semantic vector, native images, indexes) is an interpretation of it and may drop facts only because the AST remains.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n## Tuples and Datoms\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nTuples are elements in an open moduli space, graded by dimension $n$. A tuple can be any dimension/size.\\\\\\\\nA datom is the canonical persistent tuple `[e a v t m]` (entity, attribute, value, transaction, metadata). Persistent facts are immutable datoms.\\\\\\\\nSource scope belongs to the interpreter, never to an appended tuple slot. Queries keep physical sources as separate db-values and express union or joins explicitly.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nSee [`docs/design/datom.md`](./datom.md) for full specification.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n## Streams\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n`dao.stream` is an I/O monad and the system's portable I/O ABI. Functions consume streams and produce streams.\\\\\\\\nHosts may install arbitrary local functions, including direct I/O; their availability and migration semantics must be explicit.\\\\\\\\nPortable behavior cannot assume them; portable side effects appear as stream emissions. Direct-I/O host modules are explicitly local and non-migratable (owner decision, 2026-10-23).\\\\\\\\nStreams are values that can be sent through streams.\\\\\\\\nStreams carry whatever values the consumer needs: no datom requirement, no privileged payload shape.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nA stream has no privileged reader, and its meaning is not in it. Any number\\\\\\\\nof interpreters may observe the same medium, and each constructs its own\\\\\\\\nsemantics from the same batches: a `yin.vm` evaluator observing a program\\\\\\\\nstream constructs CESK state and executes it; `dao.space.index` observing\\\\\\\\nthe very same stream materializes covered indexes, so `dao.space.query/q`\\\\\\\\nanswers Datalog over the program as it grows. Neither knows the other\\\\\\\\nexists. The medium is their only coupling, and each observer's ignorance of\\\\\\\\nthe other is load-bearing: the evaluator never consults an index to run,\\\\\\\\nand the index never evaluates a form to index it. This is how axiom 2\\\\\\\\nbecomes architecture: one stream, many interpretations, none of them\\\\\\\\nthe stream's own.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nFunctions can take anything and return anything.\\\\\\\\nAgents are functions, closures, or continuations in `yin.vm` that communicate portably with the outside world through `dao.stream`. Portable agent behavior is specified through stream effects; host-local functions may also perform direct I/O.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nSee [`docs/design/dao.stream.md`](./dao.stream.md) for full stream specification.\\\\\\\\nSee [`docs/design/yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md`](./yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md) for VM architecture.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n### Host Boundaries\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nA host boundary is the dao.stream abstraction boundary; implementations do not add contracts.\\\\\\\\n[datom.world.abi.md](./datom.world.abi.md) owns the doctrine, owner rulings, taxonomy, floor and custody rules.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n## Architectural Commitments\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n- **`dao.jing` is syntax, agents are semantics**: the content-addressed encoding stays passive and payload-agnostic.\\\\\\\\n- **Macros are stream topology**: an evaluator knows nothing about macro expansion; expansion happens on the syntax side of a medium boundary, upstream of any evaluator.\\\\\\\\n- **Peer observers, one stream**: a generic evaluator and `dao.space` independently observe the same stream topic, as described under Streams. This is not a claim about any two consumers regardless of what they consume.\\\\\\\\n- **Observers are independent and optional**: nothing requires a medium to have any particular set of consumers. Which observers exist for a stream is a per-composition choice, not a design-time commitment.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n## Design Principles\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nCode quality is measured by malleability: how cheaply can changes adapt without breaking promises?\\\\\\\\nStigmergic coordination emerges from sharing data across streams.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n**Systems emerge from interpreters of pure data.** Streams are the\\\\\\\\ncomposition medium; each interpreter gives the same data a perspective\\\\\\\\nwithout collapsing interpretation into execution or owning the truth.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n- `dao.space` emerges from `dao.space.index` and `dao.space.query` over\\\\\\\\n  ordinary streams.\\\\\\\\n- ShiBi capability, metering, and budget emerge from different\\\\\\\\n  interpreters over the same tuples; no token object is stored.\\\\\\\\n- The four VMs interpret one Universal AST; many syntaxes feed that AST.\\\\\\\\n- The semantic AST in `dao.space` is queryable facts projected by the\\\\\\\\n  auto-index observer.\\\\\\\\n- Client and server are roles in one stigmergic behavior implemented by\\\\\\\\n  interpreters, not distinct kinds of nodes.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n**Derive, don't persist.** Before adding a tag, slot, or field to any\\\\\\\\ncanonical content-hashed structure (an AST grammar, a datom schema, a row\\\\\\\\nshape), check whether a correctly scoped Datalog query over\\\\\\\\n`dao.space.query/q` can already compute the fact. If it can, it belongs as a\\\\\\\\nquery or projection: new persisted structure migrates every downstream\\\\\\\\nconsumer and duplicates authority over a fact a query derives fresh. The\\\\\\\\nretired `:global` tag in\\\\\\\\n[`docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md`](./yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md) is the\\\\\\\\ncase study, including the scoping bug that a naive query introduced.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nSee [`docs/agents/malleability.md`](../agents/malleability.md) for complete design guidance (cohesion, coupling, bounded complexity, evolution, testing strategy).\\\\\\\\nSee [`docs/agents/vocabulary.md`](../agents/vocabulary.md) for domain vocabulary reference (biology, economics, physics, philosophy).\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n## Code Style\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n- Prefer simple data over rich types.\\\\\\\\n- Prefer total functions over partial ones.\\\\\\\\n- Prefer declarative pipelines over imperative logic.\\\\\\\\n- Avoid cleverness.\\\\\\\\n- Name things after what they do to streams.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n## Development Workflow\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n- Do not invent abstractions that hide streams.\\\\\\\\n- Do not suggest mainstream frameworks unless explicitly asked.\\\\\\\\n- Do not optimize prematurely.\\\\\\\\n- When uncertain, ask at the architectural level, not the implementation level.\\\\\\\\n- Explanations should align with Plan 9 / Datomic / Lisp lineage.\\\\\\\\n- Program store writes go through `engine/store-put`; any other store write\\\\\\\\n  the store-write audit (`test/yin/vm/store_write_audit_test.clj`)\\\\\\\\n  detects must be on its allowlist; its docstring states the residual it\\\\\\\\n  leaves to review.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n### Problem Decomposition\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nIf a problem appears complex:\\\\\\\\n1. Decompose it into streams.\\\\\\\\n2. Identify invariants.\\\\\\\\n3. Restrict degrees of freedom.\\\\\\\\n4. Make causality explicit.\\\\\\\\n5. Let structure emerge from constraints.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n## Operational & Specialized Guides\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n- **Advanced Concepts**: See [`docs/agents/advanced-concepts.md`](../agents/advanced-concepts.md) for parallel transport, capability tokens, and entanglement.\\\\\\\\n- **Build & Test**: See [`docs/agents/build-n-test.md`](../agents/build-n-test.md) for build, lint, and test commands and TDD guidelines.\\\\\\\\n- **Engineering Team Roster**: See [`docs/agents/team.md`](../agents/team.md) for engineering team roles and LLM model assignments.\\\\\\\\n- **Agent Delegation**: See [`docs/agents/roles/orchestrator.md`](../agents/roles/orchestrator.md) for collaboration protocols and delegating tasks or reviews through `agy`, `cmd`, `glm`, `deepseek`, and `codex`; each orchestrator session has its own append-only log at `orchestrator-logs/<unix-time-stamp>-<brief-orchestrator-descriptive-name>.md` under the repository root.\\\\\\\\n- **Website & Content**: See [`docs/agents/format.md`](../agents/format.md) for `.chp` and `.blog` EDN/Hiccup file specifications.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"exit_code\\\\\\\":0,\\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"completed\\\\\\\"}}\\\\n11:{\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"item.completed\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"item\\\\\\\":{\\\\\\\"id\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"item_4\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"command_execution\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"command\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"cat collab/yang-clojure/1795310000000-owner-ruling-occurrence-identity-unitarity.md; rg -n 'round.?4|round.?5|P1-a|P1-b|847aa885|reject' orchestrator-logs/1793385006-zcode-glm-5.3-flash-yang-clojure-track.md\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"aggregated_output\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Created-Local: 2026-11-08 2x:xx +0700\\\\\\\\nCoding-Agent: interactive (ZCode, yang.clojure orchestrator seat)\\\\\\\\nStatus: DRAFT — pending owner sign-off (ruling text from the owner's words in session; filed verbatim-in-substance below)\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n# OWNER RULING OF RECORD — occurrence identity under unitarity (resolves the codex cc1 gate reject)\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n## The owner's principle (quoted-in-substance)\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nThe compilation pipeline is transformation of values over dao.stream, so unitarity applies: **information is never lost**. The mapping is **bijective** — two things may compile to the same thing and should have the same content hash, but because of unitarity, **the complete history of the transformation is intact**. Two things may compile to the same thing and have the same hash **but have different history that can be recovered**.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n## Rulings derived (U1–U3)\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n**U1 — an occurrence is not its content address.** The occurrence identity is **(content address, origin)** — the address as reached through a specific transformation event. \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Two occurrences of identical content\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" is well-formed: they differ in history, not content. The per-occurrence origins in the call-site side table, keyed on `[origin root-address path]`, ARE the query that recovers the distinction (design §2.1/§2.3). Codex's P1-a objection (\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"identical generated/input applications share an address, so provenance cannot be classified by content\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\") is ANSWERED: provenance is not classified by content — it is read from the retained history: the expansion-event record behind a `[:expansion record-address]` origin, the source position behind a `[:source …]` origin. Never minted, never guessed from content.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n**U2 — admission identity is history-recoverable, declared by the composition.** If the composition's history contains two admission events, bijectivity demands both are recoverable — collapsing them into one deterministic token loses information and violates unitarity. If the history contains one event, minting a fresh token fabricates history — the violation in the other direction. Only the composition knows which its history contains, so the composition DECLARES the distinction when it exists (the caller-owned admission scope / explicit `:yin.link.publish/admission` token), and the deterministic content-derived default is CORRECT precisely because re-publication of identical content with no declared distinction IS one event. Codex's P1-b objection is ANSWERED on these grounds: the requirement is not \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"every admission mints a fresh token\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" (that fabricates history) but \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"every DISTINCT event is representable and recoverable\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" (the scoped/explicit mechanism provides it; the composition supplies it when its history contains two events).\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n**U3 — the general principle.** Identity is never minted from structure OR from process. Occurrence distinctness is either (i) READ from retained history (per-occurrence origins, expansion events), or (ii) DECLARED by the party whose history it is (composition-supplied admission identities). Provenance remains recoverable by address or query over datoms — axiom 2 holds in both channels.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n## Consequences\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n- The cc1 branch state (d91489f7) STANDS as the implementation of U1/U2 — no further design-level code change is required to answer the codex reject; the objection is resolved on the record by this ruling.\\\\\\\\n- The landing sequence proceeds: astra re-check against this ruling → codex re-gate → merge to master → the exception track's R3 dispatch → adoption rounds 2–4 + D1.\\\\\\\\n- Addendum A's occurrence contract is unaffected in shape: K = [origin root-address path], `:yang/call-site 1`, the occurrence/content boundary — all unchanged. U1 clarifies WHAT an origin identifies (an event in the retained history), not how it attaches.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n— drafted by the yang.clojure orchestrator seat from the owner's ruling in session\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n## Sign-off (owner, 2026-11-09 01:5x +07)\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nThe owner reviewed U1–U3 and signed off verbatim: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"sign-off\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\". Status:\\\\\\\\n**RULING OF RECORD** — the codex cc1 gate reject (P1-a/P1-b) is answered on\\\\\\\\nthe record; the landing sequence proceeds (astra re-check → codex re-gate →\\\\\\\\nmerge). Signed off by the owner in session to the master-green orchestrator\\\\\\\\nseat, witnessed and recorded here.\\\\\\\\n47:Decisions: The rejection-then-fix cycle with the architect continues to catch real defects (r1's recursion probe found a hole my own probes missed). Collection-call syntax `(v 1)/(m :k)/(k m)` — the gate round's real feature — survives; only its routing was reconciled with the convention.\\\\\\\\n90:Done: (1) The codex runner-fix round returned a29a1991: the gen:antlr trigger moved from the JVM lane's reach to the all-lane reach, docstring corrected, the affected_test expectations updated (the old test even carried a stale :gen:python-antlr expectation). Codex verified the fresh-worktree acceptance case (gen:antlr ran, four python JS files generated, cljs yang.base 34/0 after bb deps:npm — confirming the npm scratch-clone gap too), JVM yang.base 122/0, JVM dao.stream 538/0 with the skip intact, kondo 0/0. (2) This seat verified: rebased onto master (51340bf1), the focused prerequisites test green on all three lanes, and the affected-test namespace strictly better than master (13 failures = the pre-existing registry coverage issues codex recorded, vs master's 14 which include the stale expectation this fix repairs). MERGED eb27fd47, pushed; findings archived to collab/yang-clojure (the worktree's pre-commit hook correctly rejected a flat collab/ artifact — the one-record regime enforced). (3) The kernel-gate evidence refresh: the first shiva dispatch died on heredoc quoting before the JVM started; rerunning in the foreground-ish capture — the oracle suite is compiling now.\\\\\\\\n162:## 2026-11-02 22:35:00 +07 — muse quota exhausted until tomorrow; codex resumed post-reset; H3-0 round 5 dispatched there\\\\\\\\n167:Done: (1) Muse round 3 (subscription, shel