### 3.2 Arrays and keys\\\\n   112\\\\t\\\\n   113\\\\tA PHP array is a value: assignment and parameter passing copy it, and a write\\\\n   114\\\\tnever affects another holder. Its representation is chosen by what it contains\\\\n   115\\\\t(section 4.2): an array that transitively holds no reference, no\\\\n   116\\\\tarray-instance handle and no object handle is a **plain** persistent ordered\\\\n   117\\\\tmap stored directly in its holder, copied in O(1), with mutation as get, pure\\\\n   118\\\\tupdate and `cell/set!`. Any other array is a counted **instance** cell whose\\\\n   119\\\\tduplication follows the engine (section 4).\\\\n   120\\\\t\\\\n   121\\\\t- **Key coercion** at every write and read: an integer stays; a\\\\n   122\\\\t  decimal-integer string in canonical form (`\\\\\\\"8\\\\\\\"` yes; `\\\\\\\"08\\\\\\\"`, `\\\\\\\"8.0\\\\\\\"`, `\\\\\\\" 8\\\\\\\"`\\\\n   123\\\\t  no) becomes that integer; a float truncates toward zero (with the PHP 8.1\\\\n   124\\\\t  deprecation emitted as a diagnostic record); `true` and `false` become 1 and\\\\n   125\\\\t  0; `null` becomes `\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"`; any other string stays a string.\\\\n   126\\\\t- `next-index` is one more than the largest integer key ever used and never\\\\n   127\\\\t  decreases on unset; negative keys follow PHP 8.3's rule (next is max + 1).\\\\n   128\\\\t  `$a[] = v` appends at `next-index`. The one decrement is `array_pop`, which\\\\n   129\\\\t  lowers it when the popped key was `next-index - 1`.\\\\n   130\\\\t- Iteration follows the key order (`php/array-keys`; the prelude's array\\\\n   131\\\\t  operations are the only code that reads `:index`, `:order` or `:next-index`).\\\\n   132\\\\t  Unset splices the order. Nested arrays are nested\\\\n   133\\\\t  values: `$a['x']['y'] = 1` is one path update.\\\\n   134\\\\t- Large arrays cost one path copy per write; a packed-vector fast path is an\\\\n   135\\\\t  attached optimizer's business, not the lowering's.\\\\n   136\\\\t\\\\n   137\\\\t#### 3.2.1 Reads, `isset`, `empty`, `unset` and builtins (P2)\\\\n   138\\\\t\\\\n   139\\\\t- **Reads.** A missing key warns (2) `Undefined array key \\\\\\\"x\\\\\\\"` or\\\\n   140\\\\t  `Undefined array key 5` and answers null; an offset read on null, bool, int\\\\n   141\\\\t  or float warns `Trying to access array offset on <type|true|false>`; a\\\\n   142\\\\t  string container answers the one byte at the offset (negative counts from the\\\\n   143\\\\t  end), out of range warns `Uninitialized string offset N` and answers `\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"`.\\\\n   144\\\\t  `$a[]` in read position is a compile error `Cannot use [] for reading`.\\\\n   145\\\\t- **`isset`/`empty` on a string container** coerce the offset, then test the\\\\n   146\\\\t  range: int itself; `true`, `false`, null as `1`, `0`, `0` silently; a float\\\\n   147\\\\t  by the float-key seam (`php/float-key`, precision deprecation when\\\\n   148\\\\t  fractional, no \\\\\\\"String offset cast\\\\\\\" warning, which belongs to reads); an\\\\n   149\\\\t  integer-numeric string as that int; anything else is not an offset (`isset`\\\\n   150\\\\t  false, `empty` true).\\\\n   151\\\\t- **`unset` on a non-array**: a missing key or null at any level is a no-op;\\\\n   152\\\\t  a string terminal is `Error \\\\\\\"Cannot unset string offsets\\\\\\\"`; int, float and\\\\n   153\\\\t  `true` are `Error \\\\\\\"Cannot unset offset in a non-array variable\\\\\\\"`; `false`\\\\n   154\\\\t  emits Deprecated (8192) `Automatic conversion of false to array is\\\\n   155\\\\t  deprecated` and stays `false`.\\\\n   156\\\\t- **Builtin protocol.** One table (`yang.php.antlr.builtins/supported`) feeds\\\\n   157\\\\t  the prelude registration and the lowering's by-reference positions. For a\\\\n   158\\\\t  call with `:by-ref` positions the lowering (1) binds each by-ref location's\\\\n   159\\\\t  keys at that argument's place in left-to-right order; (2) evaluates every\\\\n   160\\\\t  other argument left to right into temporaries (a non-location at a by-ref\\\\n   161\\\\t  position warns 8 `Only variables should be passed by reference`); (3)\\\\n   162\\\\t  immediately before the call, reads each by-ref location through its bound\\\\n   163\\\\t  keys and builds the argument vector; (4) calls; (5) writes each out back\\\\n   164\\\\t  through the same keys, in position order.\\\\n   165\\\\t- **`foreach` by value** iterates a snapshot (`php/foreach-array`, then\\\\n   166\\\\t  `php/array-keys` and `php/array-get`), so writes, `unset`, appends and\\\\n   167\\\\t  `sort` of the source in the body never change the iteration (the by-value\\\\n   168\\\\t  half of C15). A non-array warns and iterates nothing.\\\\n   169\\\\t- **Sorting** is one stable merge sort in the prelude (no host sort), with\\\\n   170\\\\t  flags `SORT_REGULAR`, `SORT_NUMERIC`, `SORT_STRING`; `r*` variants flip the\\\\n   171\\\\t  sign without reversing equal elements. A **bool** comparator is retried\\\\n   172\\\\t  swapped and emits Deprecated 8192 `usort(): Returning bool from comparison\\\\n   173\\\\t  function is deprecated, return an integer less than, equal to, or greater\\\\n   174\\\\t  than zero` once per call. Parity claim: `usort` with a bool comparator\\\\n   175\\\\t  `return $a > $b;` over `[3, 1, 2]` matches the CLI's order and emits the\\\\n   176\\\\t  deprecation exactly once per call. Stability is not parity for a comparator\\\\n   177\\\\t  that disagrees with itself (corner G-sort-inconsistent-cmp, section 17.2).\\\\n   178\\\\t\\\\n   179\\\\t### 3.3 Scope\\\\n   180\\\\t\\\\n   181\\\\t- PHP has function scope only, with no block scope and no implicit closure\\\\n   182\\\\t  capture, except arrow `fn`, which captures by value every enclosing variable\\\\n   183\\\\t  it mentions, at creation.\\\\n   184\\\\t- **Locals are cells** allocated at function entry with `:php/undef`; reading\\\\n   185\\\\t  one emits `Warning: Undefined variable` and yields null. Every function body\\\\n   186\\\\t  that declares locals or parameters releases them on exit (row 16).\\\\n   187\\\\t- `global $x` binds the local to the `$GLOBALS` entry by reference (row 17),\\\\n   188\\\\t  creating it as null if absent.\\\\n   189\\\\t- `static $x = <expr>;` is a cell owned by the function object, allocated at\\\\n   190\\\\t  function creation with an uninitialized sentinel. Its initializer is an\\\\n   191\\\\t  arbitrary expression evaluated **when execution first reaches the\\\\n   192\\\\t  declaration** (PHP 8.3) and filled once.\\\\n   193\\\\t- **The dynamic-scope profile**: a function whose body contains\\\\n   194\\\\t  `include`/`require`, `$$name`, `extract`, `compact`, `get_defined_vars`,\\\\n   195\\\\t  `eval`, or one-argument `parse_str` keeps its locals in a heap symbol-table\\\\n   196\\\\t  dict (the precedent is Python's module namespace, owner decision 1 of\\\\n   197\\\\t  `yang.antlr.md` 8.5.1). Scope analysis decides per function, and the\\\\n   198\\\\t  lowering targets `php/local-get`/`php/local-set` on the dict. All other\\\\n   199\\\\t  functions use cells (owner decision B8.3). The symbol table holds reference\\\\n   200\\\\t  markers like any location.\\\\n   201\\\\t- **The global scope** is always the `$GLOBALS` dict: a heap dict,\\\\n   202\\\\t  insertion-ordered, with the superglobals pre-seeded from the request record.\\\\n   203\\\\t  `$GLOBALS` is read-only as of PHP 8.1 except through its elements (corner\\\\n   204\\\\t  C13).\\\\n   205\\\\t\\\\n   206\\\\t### 3.4 Superglobals and the request context\\\\n   207\\\\t\\\\n   208\\\\t- A PHP run is one task consuming one **request record** from a\\\\n   209\\\\t  composition-supplied stream: `{:php.request/method :uri :query :headers\\\\n   210\\\\t  :cookies :body :server :env :files}`. `php/init!` builds `$_GET`, `$_POST`,\\\\n   350\\\\t### 3.11 Mixed HTML mode\\\\n   351\\\\t\\\\n   352\\\\t`inlineHtml` tokens lower to `php/echo` of the literal text in source order\\\\n   353\\\\tbetween code blocks. `?>` swallows one following newline (the lexer's rule,\\\\n   354\\\\tpreserved through the span); the first `<?php` has no output before it unless\\\\n   355\\\\tthere is text. Output order is statement order: exit criterion 1 is a direct\\\\n   356\\\\tfixture.\\\\n   357\\\\t\\\\n   358\\\\t### 3.12 eval\\\\n   359\\\\t\\\\n   360\\\\t`eval` is a new compilation request (`yang.antlr.md` 8.6) sharing the caller's\\\\n   361\\\\tscope, so it forces the dynamic-scope profile on its function. It is refused\\\\n   362\\\\twith `:yang.php/unsupported :eval` until its slice (P9).\\\\n   363\\\\t\\\\n   364\\\\t## 4. The reference, array and object lifecycle\\\\n   365\\\\t\\\\n   366\\\\tPHP's observable aliasing (references, copy-on-write separation that unwraps\\\\n   367\\\\tunshared references, destructor timing) is defined by the Zend engine's\\\\n   368\\\\toperations, so this model is derived from them, not from an abstraction. Each\\\\n   369\\\\trow names the Zend handler it maps to; each row is normative and is confirmed\\\\n   370\\\\tagainst the pinned PHP CLI before the slice that claims it lands (the blocking\\\\n   371\\\\trule, section 17.3).\\\\n   372\\\\t\\\\n   373\\\\t### 4.1 What the engine does\\\\n   374\\\\t\\\\n   375\\\\t- A **zval** is a typed slot in a CV, a hashtable bucket, a property table or\\\\n  1400\\\\tlambdas with cells, which also makes safepoints and tail marks work.\\\\n  1401\\\\t\\\\n  1402\\\\tMigration mechanics (the F2 pattern):\\\\n  1403\\\\t\\\\n  1404\\\\t- The ANTLR frontend is installed in `yin.repl.frontends/standard` as a\\\\n  1405\\\\t  new revision of `:yang/php` (the compiler frontend keeps its revision\\\\n  1406\\\\t  during the migration window; `[id revision]` pins either), with no\\\\n  1407\\\\t  fallback.\\\\n  1408\\\\t- Every program in `test/yang/php_test.clj` is re-expressed in the new corpus,\\\\n  1409\\\\t  with expectations corrected where the legacy was wrong (`null` is\\\\n  1410\\\\t  `:php/null`, not `nil`).\\\\n  1411\\\\t- The browser demo `src/cljs/datomworld/demo/compilation_pipeline.cljs`\\\\n  1412\\\\t  compiles editable PHP synchronously in CLJS with the legacy frontend, while\\\\n  1413\\\\t  the local ANTLR parser is JVM-only until a Dart helper exists (amended\\\\n  1414\\\\t  2026-10-17: PHP has Java and JavaScript helpers upstream; per-target helper\\\\n  1415\\\\t  availability decides the host matrix, `yang.antlr.md` 4.2). The demo moves to **checked-in CST packets**\\\\n  1416\\\\t  for its sample programs (generated by a JVM script, the `dht-import-v1.edn`\\\\n  1417\\\\t  pattern), lowered and run parserless in the browser; editable PHP source in\\\\n  1418\\\\t  the demo is dropped, or routed to a configured parser service when one\\\\n  1419\\\\t  exists (owner decision B8.9). `src/dev/subsystems.edn` is a one-line\\\\n  1420\\\\t  retarget.\\\\n  1421\\\\t- The compiler revision of `:yang/php` retires at P8 (implementation\\\\n  1422\\\\t  deleted, no shim; the repository has no backward-compatibility\\\\n  1423\\\\t  obligation) once the demo retarget has landed and the corpus covers the\\\\n  1424\\\\t  compiler's programs (owner decision B8.6).\\\\n  1425\\\\t\\\\n  1426\\\\tWhat stays refused after migration: `goto`; return by reference (corner C7);\\\\n  1427\\\\t`declare(encoding)`; `__halt_compiler`; `eval` until its slice; `$$` and\\\\n  1428\\\\t`extract` until the dynamic-scope slice; extension surfaces (`ext/*`: PDO,\\\\n  1429\\\\tcurl, mbstring, gd, intl, openssl, sockets, `pcntl` beyond signals), each a\\\\n  1430\\\\tbound surface per ADR 0004; `preg_*` until a portable regex engine exists (a\\\\n  1431\\\\tPCRE-compatible engine is large and host engines diverge); `date`, `time`,\\\\n  1432\\\\t`microtime`, `rand`, `random_int` and `uniqid` exist only as effects with\\\\n  1433\\\\texplicit outcomes.\\\\n  1434\\\\t\\\\n  1435\\\\t## 6. The grammar route and parser worker\\\\n  1590\\\\t|            | methods; constructors with     |                                |\\\\n  1591\\\\t|            | promotion;                     |                                |\\\\n  1592\\\\t|            | `static`/`self`/`parent`;      |                                |\\\\n  1593\\\\t|            | interfaces; traits (`use`,     |                                |\\\\n  1594\\\\t|            | `insteadof`, `as`); enums      |                                |\\\\n  1595\\\\t|            | (pure and backed); attributes  |                                |\\\\n  1596\\\\t|            | (data only); magic methods;    |                                |\\\\n  1597\\\\t|            | `__toString` in string         |                                |\\\\n  1598\\\\t|            | contexts                       |                                |\\\\n  1599\\\\t+------------+--------------------------------+--------------------------------+\\\\n  1600\\\\t| Exceptions | `try`/`catch`/`finally`,       | handler stack with             |\\\\n  1601\\\\t|            | multi-catch, `throw`           | `php/raise`; `finally` via     |\\\\n  1602\\\\t|            | expressions, rethrow           | `php/try-finally`              |\\\\n  1603\\\\t+------------+--------------------------------+--------------------------------+\\\\n  1604\\\\t| Generators | `yield`, `yield k => v`,       | the C2 core under `php/gen-*`  |\\\\n  1605\\\\t|            | `yield from`; `send`, `throw`, |                                |\\\\n  1606\\\\t|            | `getReturn`                    |                                |\\\\n  1607\\\\t+------------+--------------------------------+--------------------------------+\\\\n  1608\\\\t| Modules    | `include`, `require`, `_once`; | section 11; ticks per section  |\\\\n  1609\\\\t|            | `namespace`, `use`;            | 12                             |\\\\n  1610\\\\t|            | `declare(strict_types)`,       |                     